00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.898
LTX 2.5 just landed on Hugging Face,

00:00:01.898 --> 00:00:04.610
so I gave it the same AI influencer prompts I

00:00:04.610 --> 00:00:05.424
give every model.

00:00:05.424 --> 00:00:07.051
And honestly, the results surprised me,

00:00:07.051 --> 00:00:09.220
because some clips look better than I expected.

00:00:09.220 --> 00:00:11.119
And the LTX prompting skill I used,

00:00:11.119 --> 00:00:13.017
to have optomized prompts for this model,

00:00:13.017 --> 00:00:15.729
you get complete for free, at the end of this

00:00:15.729 --> 00:00:16.000
video.

00:00:16.000 --> 00:00:19.478
Okay, let's get straight into it.

00:00:19.478 --> 00:00:23.536
Everything here is a real screen recording,

00:00:23.536 --> 00:00:27.014
so you can follow every step.

00:00:27.014 --> 00:00:31.072
We grab LTX 2.5 from Hugging Face,

00:00:31.072 --> 00:00:36.870
load the standard ComfyUI workflow, and run my fixed AI

00:00:36.870 --> 00:00:42.087
influencer prompts, the same set I give every model.

00:00:42.087 --> 00:00:47.884
And the prompts and the settings stay on screen the

00:00:47.884 --> 00:00:53.681
whole time, because this is about building an AI influencer,

00:00:53.681 --> 00:00:56.000
not a cinematic demo.

00:00:56.000 --> 00:00:57.667
This is the official page, Lightricks LTX-2.5 on Hugging Face.

00:00:57.667 --> 00:00:59.000
The weights are gated behind agree and access,

00:00:59.000 --> 00:01:00.667
under the LTX-2 community license, so it's an open weights

00:01:00.667 --> 00:01:00.833
model.

00:01:00.833 --> 00:01:02.500
Inside you get a 22 Billion dev model and a

00:01:02.500 --> 00:01:03.833
distilled one, ComfyUI variants, a Gemma text encoder,

00:01:03.833 --> 00:01:04.833
and separate video and audio VAEs,

00:01:04.833 --> 00:01:05.833
so it can generate sound too.

00:01:05.833 --> 00:01:07.500
And the positioning changed, because Lightricks framed 2.3 around a

00:01:07.500 --> 00:01:08.500
rebuilt video VAE and finer detail,

00:01:08.500 --> 00:01:09.833
and they frame 2.5 around continuity, control, efficiency,

00:01:09.833 --> 00:01:10.833
stronger prompt understanding, and native multi-shot.

00:01:10.833 --> 00:01:11.833
That's their words, not our test,

00:01:11.833 --> 00:01:13.167
and continuity is the part an influencer needs,

00:01:13.167 --> 00:01:14.000
so we test exactly that.

00:01:14.000 --> 00:01:16.692
So these are the three official LTX 2.5 workflows:

00:01:16.692 --> 00:01:18.486
text to video, image to video,

00:01:18.486 --> 00:01:20.579
and first and last frame to video.

00:01:20.579 --> 00:01:23.570
For an AI influencer, image to video is the one

00:01:23.570 --> 00:01:24.168
that matters.

00:01:24.168 --> 00:01:27.159
We feed it the same character image and see whether

00:01:27.159 --> 00:01:28.355
her identity survives motion.

00:01:28.355 --> 00:01:31.346
For this test, I'm using the official workflow at 0.9

00:01:31.346 --> 00:01:33.439
megapixels in 9 to 16 aspect ratio.

00:01:33.439 --> 00:01:35.832
For five seconds at 24 frames per second,

00:01:35.832 --> 00:01:37.028
with prompt enhancement enabled.

00:01:37.028 --> 00:01:40.019
And i've put all three ComfyUI workflows in my Discord,

00:01:40.019 --> 00:01:41.813
the link is in the description.

00:01:41.813 --> 00:01:44.804
Now let's run our fixed influencer prompts and look at

00:01:44.804 --> 00:01:46.000
what actually comes back.

00:01:46.000 --> 00:01:47.333
And this is Lina.

00:01:47.333 --> 00:01:50.667
If you've watched this channel before, you already know her.

00:01:50.667 --> 00:01:54.000
For this benchmark, she gets two fixed reference images and

00:01:54.000 --> 00:01:57.333
two fixed tests, and every future video model gets the

00:01:57.333 --> 00:01:58.333
exact same inputs.

00:01:58.333 --> 00:02:01.000
The first image is a controlled studio portrait.

00:02:01.000 --> 00:02:03.000
Test one is the expression test:

00:02:03.000 --> 00:02:06.333
the framing stays locked while her face moves from neutral

00:02:06.333 --> 00:02:08.000
into a clearly different expression.

00:02:08.000 --> 00:02:11.000
We are watching the eyes, mouth, teeth, skin, hair,

00:02:11.000 --> 00:02:14.333
and whether she still looks like Lina at the end.

00:02:14.333 --> 00:02:17.667
And this is what LTX 2.5 produced on the very

00:02:17.667 --> 00:02:18.333
first generation.

00:02:18.333 --> 00:02:20.333
Honestly, for an open-source video model,

00:02:20.333 --> 00:02:22.333
I think this is extremely good.

00:02:22.333 --> 00:02:25.000
The expression develops naturally, her identity stays consistent,

00:02:25.000 --> 00:02:27.000
and even the teeth hold up.

00:02:27.000 --> 00:02:30.333
The only instruction it missed was that the camera should

00:02:30.333 --> 00:02:33.333
stay completely locked with no zoom in the prompt.

00:02:33.333 --> 00:02:36.000
Instead, it slowly pushes in throughout the shot.

00:02:36.000 --> 00:02:38.000
So the visual quality is strong,

00:02:38.000 --> 00:02:40.333
but the instruction following is not perfect.

00:02:40.333 --> 00:02:43.333
Reference two is the opposite: a real influencer-style selfie,

00:02:43.333 --> 00:02:46.667
with a high camera angle, a peace sign close to

00:02:46.667 --> 00:02:49.000
the lens and a difficult body pose.

00:02:49.000 --> 00:02:52.000
The movement test asks LTX to coordinate her body,

00:02:52.000 --> 00:02:55.000
arm, hand, and hair without breaking the selfie perspective,

00:02:55.000 --> 00:02:57.333
her identity, or the room around her.

00:02:57.333 --> 00:03:00.667
And this is what LTX 2.5 produced on the very

00:03:00.667 --> 00:03:01.333
first generation.

00:03:01.333 --> 00:03:04.667
This is a genuinely hard stress test for the model,

00:03:04.667 --> 00:03:08.000
because there are so many things happening in one prompt

00:03:08.000 --> 00:03:10.667
and one five-second video: body movement, facial movement,

00:03:10.667 --> 00:03:13.333
the hand, individual fingers, hair interaction, camera motion,

00:03:13.333 --> 00:03:16.333
and even the reflection in the window behind her.

00:03:16.333 --> 00:03:19.333
And LTX 2.5 handles most of it surprisingly well.

00:03:19.333 --> 00:03:22.667
The fingers stay very realistic, and when she runs her

00:03:22.667 --> 00:03:26.000
hand through her hair, both the hand movement and the

00:03:26.000 --> 00:03:28.333
way the hair reacts look extremely convincing.

00:03:28.333 --> 00:03:31.667
I also like that the reflection behind her stays present

00:03:31.667 --> 00:03:33.000
as the scene moves.

00:03:33.000 --> 00:03:35.667
The only obvious weakness is near the end,

00:03:35.667 --> 00:03:38.667
where her eyes start to look a little strange.

00:03:38.667 --> 00:03:42.000
But overall, for an open-source model handling this much movement

00:03:42.000 --> 00:03:44.667
at once, this is a very strong result.

